FROM SEED TO SEEDLING  (8 s; 1 scenes, 1 shots)
synopsis: A dry red kidney bean sits partly planted in perlite, with its hard coat and hilum visible. In compressed growth, it absorbs moisture, swells, and progressively loosens its coat without the coat disappearing. A root extends downward and branches while the cotyledons enlarge and the first leaves begin emerging. The shoot rises and unfolds two unifoliate leaves, ending on a young seedling with a visible terminal growing tip and developing roots below.
theme: Growth becomes understandable when its hidden beginnings remain visible.

1. INT. GERMINATION SETUP — DAY   0–8 s
   synopsis: A dry kidney bean sits partly embedded in perlite with space above it and an exposed root zone below. Compressed germination reveals water uptake, downward root development, and upward shoot development, ending with two unfolded unifoliate leaves and a visible terminal growing tip.
   situation: A dry red kidney bean is partly planted in perlite at an exposed root-zone face under unseen overhead illumination; its growth is compressed into eight seconds against an undecorated cyan-blue background.
   stage: Origin at the planting point on the perlite surface. x runs horizontally toward screen right; y runs rearward into the growing area; z runs upward, with z 0 at the planting surface. The camera is on negative y. The kidney-bean position identifies its fixed planting reference throughout growth. The fixed 16:9 image covers approximately x -0.089 to 0.089 and z -0.035 to 0.065.; x toward none
   tone: Patient, clear, and quietly hopeful.
   ambience: Very quiet indoor room tone, with no distinct growth sounds.
   line: The kidney-bean planting point and the centre of the perlite bed along the y axis.; camera side: The exposed front side of the growing area, at negative y.
   blocking: kidney-bean: Partly embedded at the exposed front surface of the perlite, with its initial centre at x 0, y 0, z 0.002.; facing No intrinsic front; the hilum-bearing side faces the exposed viewing side of the perlite.; holding nothing | perlite: Stationary growing medium surrounding and below the bean; its exposed upper surface meets the bean at z 0, and its front face leaves the developing root zone visible.; facing None.; holding nothing

   SHOT 1  0–8 s  close  (scene start)  pace: slow
   moment: The opposing directions of early growth are visible together.
   treatment: Clarity through one locked widescreen live-action macro composition, using photoreal growth animation without dissolves, reframing, or diagrammatic additions.
   motion: crisp frozen detail
   camera: stands Directly in front of the exposed perlite root zone, on its open viewing side, with the lens level and square to the growing area.; from the side; eye height; looks at The complete germination area, keeping the initial bean, the upward shoot space, and the downward root space inside one fixed composition.; behind: plain cyan-blue painted wall
   FIRST FRAME
      kidney-bean [in frame]  Centre of the composition at the perlite surface, with reserved growing space above and exposed root-zone space below.; large; at (0, 0, 0.002) hdg 0°; facing None; the seed has no intrinsic front.; A dry red kidney bean approximately 0.015 metres long and 0.009 metres high, its lower portion embedded in perlite. The hard red coat is intact, with a clearly resolved pale hilum on the side facing the camera. No root, stem, cotyledon interior, or leaf is exposed.
      perlite     [in frame]  Across the lower portion of the composition, surrounding the planting point and forming the visible root-zone background.; tiny; at (0, 0.018, -0.018) hdg 0°; facing None.; Stationary irregular white porous granules, lightly moist without visible free water; an exposed front root-zone face and an upper planting surface at z 0. The granules retain the same positions throughout.
      relations: kidney-bean is partly embedded at the exposed upper front surface of perlite
   ACTION
      [0s] During seconds 0–2, animate moisture uptake through continuous swelling of the initially dry bean. Its red coat becomes taut, then visibly loosens along its seam; the coat remains present and attached. Keep the hilum visible. Do not introduce visible water droplets or a watering action.
      [2s] During seconds 2–5, the radicle emerges beside the hilum and extends downward along the exposed perlite face. Short lateral roots begin branching from it. The cotyledons enlarge and begin rising as the hypocotyl extends. Keep the first leaves enclosed initially, then let their tips begin appearing alongside the cotyledons near the end of this interval. The red coat separates progressively but remains attached.
      [5s] During seconds 5–8, the upright stem extends above the cotyledons. Two opposite unifoliate leaves push upward, unfold, and expand to either side, exposing the terminal growing tip between and slightly above them. Maintain the downward primary root and its visible lateral roots below. Finish with the entire young seedling visible and the loosened red coat still attached near the cotyledons.
   NARRATION
